.TH signing-milter 8 "July, 2020"
.\"
.SH NAME
signing-milter \- sign email via milter protocol
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B signing-milter
.RI [ options ]
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B signing-milter
uses the
.I milter
interface, originally distributed as part of version 8.11 of
.B sendmail(8),
to provide signing service for mail transiting a milter-aware MTA.
Messages are signed if the RFC5321.MailFrom is found in a signing-table,
certs are present and usable and a header
.I X-Skip-Signing
does not exist.
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a authsigningtable
Enable the local CDB auth-signing table.
.I authsigningtable
is a
.I cdb
compiled database that maps an authenticated SMTP/SASL identity to one or more
permitted signing identities.
Each key (left side) is the exact, case-sensitive authenticated identity (the
SASL login name, as provided by the libmilter macro
.IR {auth_authen} );
the value (right side) is a comma or whitespace separated list of signing
identities.
Email addresses in the value are normalized (lower case, angle brackets removed)
before comparison; the authenticated identity is treated as an opaque string and
is not normalized.
Authenticated signing is only permitted when either
.B \-a
or
.B \-R
is configured; without an auth-signing-table an authenticated principal is not
authorized to use any signing identity.
When the auth-signing-table is configured, a message is only signed if the
authenticated identity is authorized for the signing identity selected from the
envelope sender or the
.B X-Signer
header (if \-f is enabled).
Messages with an unauthorized or missing authentication identity are not signed.
.TP
.B \-h
Show summary of options and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Show version of program and exit.
.TP
.B \-c clientgroup
If socket is
.I [unix|local]:path
.I signing-milter
will adjust
.I path
to be read/writeable by group
.I clientgroup.
The group must be any group other then root and the group
.I signing-milter
runs as. The parameter is required unless using an TCP socket.
The special value
.B :relax
sets the socket's group to root and mode to 0666 (world-writable).
This is insecure and is intended only for local testing; it must not be
used for a milter that has access to private signing keys.
.TP
.B \-d loglevel
Set the loglevel. The default level 3 logs all errors. Valid values are
effective syslog priorities as described in
.IR /usr/include/syslog.h
.TP
.B \-f
normally
.I signing-milter
uses the envelope sender as signeraddress to lookup in the signingtable.
If enabled,
.I signing-milter
uses also the value of a header named
.B X-Signer
to lookup the signeraddress. This is useful when using VERP
(Variable Envelope Return Path).
.TP
.B \-g group
Set the group
.I signing-milter
will run as if started as root. If omitted the groupname signing-milter is used.
.TP
.B \-k directory
for debugging purposes
.I signing-milter
can store data to files. They are created in the named directory which must be
readable, writeable an executable by the user
.I signing-milter
runs as. It must not have any rights for other.
.I signing-milter
creates two files containing unsigned and signed data. The files are named
plain-QUEUEID and signed-QUEUEID. If omitted no files are written.
.TP
.B \-l all Logs to STDOUT
Some environments work better if any logging is sent to STDOUT. This switch let
.I signing-milter
write any logmessage to STDOUT. Nothing will be send via SYSLOG, /dev/log is
not needed.
.TP
.B \-m signingtable
.I signing-milter
uses a table to lookup corresponding signingmaterial to a given sender address.
This table is a
.I cdb
compiled database. The signingtable lists mailaddresses on the left (key), one
ore more spaces or tabs and a full path to an associates file (value). These
file must contain a signer certificate and a signer private key in PEM format.
.sp
The files containing certificates and private keys are security sensitive.
Therefore they must only be readable and not writeable for the user
.I signing-milter
runs as. Also they must not be world readable.
.sp
Feature available since version 20120731:
.br
If you put cert and key in a file named /path/to/foo-cert+key.pem then
.I signing-milter
also try to load /path/to/foo-chain.pem. If it exist it should contain
intermediate and root certificates that will be included in the signature.

NOTE:
.br
There is not special error signaled if /path/to/foo-chain.pem exist
but is unreadable for the user
.I
signing-milter
run as.
When using chain certificates you should check the logs if it is really used.
.TP
.B \-n modetable
.I signing-milter
can use this optional table to lookup recipient addresses. If at least one
recipient of a message is found in modetable, the result is applied to the
whole message.
.sp
The value may contain any combination of the following words:
.RS
.TP
.B keep
for debugging purposes keep the plain and signed data in the directory given
with option
.I \-k
as plain-$queueid and signed-$queueid.  If no directory is configured with
.I \-k,
the keep action is ignored.  Files are created with owner-only permissions
(0600) and without following symbolic links.
.TP
.B opaque
the signingmode is switched from detached to opaque. Opaque mode is intended
to preserve signatures for mail transiting broken MTAs.
.TP
.B skip
message will not be signed and simply pass unchanged.
.RE
.TP
.B \-P prefix
When using the Redis certificate backend (\-r) or the Redis auth-signing table
(\-R), prepend the given
.I prefix
to every Redis key. The default is
.IR signing-milter: .
.TP
.B \-p file
Read the Redis password from
.IR file . The file must be a regular file owned by
root or the milter user, must not be a symbolic link, and must not be readable
or writable by group or other. A trailing newline is stripped. The password must
not appear in the Redis URI or on the command line.
.TP
.B \-R
Enable the optional Redis auth-signing table.  When enabled, the authenticated
identity is looked up in Redis using
.I SISMEMBER
on the set at
.IR <prefix>auth:<identity> .
Set members must be stored in canonical (lowercase, no angle brackets) signer
form, because the comparison is done by Redis, not by the milter.
The authenticated identity is used verbatim as the Redis key and is not
normalized.
A message is only signed if the authenticated identity is a member of that set
for the selected signing identity.
This option requires \-r.
.TP
.B \-r uri
Enable the optional Redis certificate backend. When enabled,
.I signing-milter
queries Redis for a certificate before falling back to the CDB signingtable.
The
.I uri
has one of the following forms:
.IR rediss://host:port/db ,
or
.IR unix:///path/to/redis.sock .
Plaintext TCP
.IR redis://
is rejected; network connections to Redis must be TLS.
Passwords must not appear in the URI; use
.B \-p
or
.B \-C
instead.
.PP
For
.IR rediss:// ,
the connection is wrapped in TLS 1.2 or newer.  By default the server
certificate is validated against the system or configured CA store, the
certificate chain is checked, and the URI host is verified against the server's
certificate.
.IR verify=peer
performs full chain and hostname/IP verification (this is the default and only
supported mode); TLS connections without peer verification are rejected.
.IR verify_name=hostname
overrides the identity that is verified; the default is the URI host.
.IR sni=hostname
sets the TLS SNI extension only and does not affect verification; the default
SNI is the verified identity if it is a DNS name.
.IR cacert=/path/to/ca.pem
and
.IR capath=/path/to/cadir
override the CA store.
.IR cert=/path/to/client-cert.pem
and
.IR key=/path/to/client-key.pem
are used for mutual TLS.
The
.IR cert
file may contain the leaf client certificate followed by any intermediate
certificates required to build a trust chain.
TLS parameters are rejected for
.IR redis:// .
IPv6 hosts must be bracketed:
.IR rediss://[::1]:6380/0 .
Unknown or duplicate TLS parameters are rejected at startup.
.TP
.B \-C file
Read Redis username and password from
.IR file .
The file must contain up to two non-empty lines: the first line is the username
and the second line is the password. If only one line is present, it is treated
as the password. The same ownership and permission requirements as for
.B \-p
apply. The credentials must not appear in the Redis URI or on the command line.
.TP
.B \-W file
Read the static passphrase for encrypted private keys from
.IR file .
The passphrase is applied when loading private keys from Redis.
A trailing newline is stripped.
.TP
.B \-s socket
Specifies the socket that should be established by the filter to receive
connections from
.I sendmail(8)
in order to provide service.
.I socket
is in one of three forms:
.I [unix|local]:path
which creates a UNIX domain socket at the specified path.
.I inet:port[@host]
which creates a TCP socket on the specified port.
.I inet6:port[@host]
which creates a TCPv6 socket on the specified port. If the host is not given as
either a hostname or an IP address, the socket will be listening on all
interfaces. If no socket is given at all, the milter listen on
.I inet6:30053@[::1].
Let's push IPv6, yeah!
.TP
.B \-t timeout
This is the number of seconds
.I signing-milter
will wait for an MTA communication (read or write) before timing out.
.TP
.B \-u user
Set the user
.I signing-milter
will run as if started under root. If omitted the username signing-milter is
used.
.TP
.B \-x
Causes
.I signing-milter
to add a header indicating the presence of this filter in the path of the
message from injection to delivery. The productname, version and hostname
are included in the headers contents.
.\"
.SH SECURITY
The
.I signing-milter
is security-sensitive. It talks to an MTA and has access to otherwise
unprotected private keys. It must run at fixed low privilege. The
.I signing-milter
refuses to run with root privileges.
.P
When the auth-signing-table (\-a or \-R) is used, do not attach
.I signing-milter
to an Internet-facing MX listening on port 25.  Use it only on authenticated
submission services, and combine it with Postfix
.IR smtpd_sender_login_maps (5)
and
.I reject_authenticated_sender_login_mismatch
so an authenticated client cannot use an envelope sender it does not own.
Otherwise unauthorized senders may be able to obtain S/MIME signatures for
addresses they do not control.
.\"
.SH ACCOUNTS
You may start signing-milter as root. In this case
.I signing-milter
will switch to user/group signing-milter or the username given as \-u and \-g.
You may also switch to a unprivileged user and then start signing-milter.
.\"
.SH LOCAL SOCKET
If you want
.I signing-milter
run with a local unixsocket it is important the socket is accessible by
.I sendmail(8).
In this case start
.I signing-milter
under root, set \-u and \-g (or leave them default) and set \-c to a group
other then root and signing-milter.
.I signing-milter
will adjust the sockets owner, group and file mode to allow proper access by
.I sendmail(8).
.\"
.SH SIGNALS
.I signing-milter
dumps statistical data on SIGALRM. The data contains the number of signed
messages and the total signingtime in seconds.milliseconds separated by /. All
counters are set to zero after SIGALRM. Also on SIGHUP and SIGTERM statistical
data are logged before terminating the daemon.
.\"
.SH STARTUP AND LOGGING
.I signing-milter
is designed to run by a supervisor like daemontools or runit. It does not fork
as daemon in background. Unless option \-l is given, all logging goes to syslog
using facility LOG_MAIL. All messages except priority LOG_NOTICE and LOG_INFO
are also sent to stdout.
.\"
.SH BUGS
.I signing-milter
should also support GnuPG.  A changed signingtable is noticed but not reloaded.
.I signing-milter
must currently restart when the signingtable has changed. Same applies to the
modetable.
.P
When using the Redis backend, certificates and private keys are fetched from
Redis and stored only in memory while the message is being signed.
A changed Redis dataset is noticed on the next per-thread lookup. Redis keys
containing signing material should be treated as confidential.
.\"
